  "query_preview": "..\n\nSurface cold front begins the process of dropping through the region \nthis evening maintaining scattered convection across the north for \nthe early evening before shifting south for the latter half. \nAvailable instability decreases through the rest of the day (MLCAPE \nfalling from ~2000 J/kg towards 1400 J/kg) resulting in lessening \ncoverage as the evening progresses. Coverage is expected to be more \nisolated to widely scattered in nature once the front reaches the \nsouthern half of the area late evening- partly from arrival late in \nthe diurnal window and partly from drier air in place (dewpoints in \nthe lower 60s vs the northern upper 60s). Any strong to severe storm \nchance remains isolated at best as MLCAPE falls sub 1500 J/kg with \nshear values holding around 25-30kts. \n\nFrontal passage ushers in a markedly cooler airmass as 850mb temps \nfall from mid teens to mid single digits (C) overnight dropping lows \ntoward the lower 50s. Upper troughing and associated thermal \ntroughing settles across the Great Lakes for the midweek period \nleading to seasonably cool temperatures. Coolest day of the week is \nWednesday where highs only reach the lower 70s with overnight lows \naround 50, some 10-15 degrees below normal for late July. Trough \naxis shifts east over southern Ontario by Thursday allowing for \nslight warming (850mb in the upper single digits C) supporting high \ntemps closer to the mid/upper 70s. \n\nWarm advection eventually redevelops Friday as quasi-zonal mid-upper \nflow expands across the Great Lakes allowing highs to return around \n80. Building upper ridging over the central CONUS this weekend \ndrives a weak periphery shortwave into the Great Lakes late Saturday \nnight-Sunday offering the area's next shot seeing some scattered \nrain. Upper 80s also look to make a return late weekend/early next \nweek as upper ridging builds east into the central Great Lakes.",
